    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start for the students to get the gist of it. I'm a bit worried, though, that some of the more conservative students might argue that this is just \"judicial activism\" and that the court is making up rights that aren't actually in the text. How should I explain why the substantive due process approach is the more legitimate way to interpret the Constitution compared to just sticking to the literal text or procedural rules?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You can explain that substantive due process is necessary because a strict \"originalist\" or literal interpretation of the Constitution would have left modern human rights like voting for all races or equal protection for women completely unaddressed. The dissenting opinion often argues that if we only followed the specific text written in the 1700s, we would be unable to adapt the Constitution to the rapidly changing social values of our time. Essentially, substantive due process acts as a legal safety valve to ensure the Constitution's core promise of liberty remains relevant to current generations of Americans.",
      "source": "target",
